WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.770
- Well, welcome everyone to

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.080
the Lotteries and Democracy Conference.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.750
I'm generously sponsored by Yale ISPS.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:16.320
We know that many of you have
traveled great distances from

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.900
Europe and the other side
of the country to be here,

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.750
and we're really, really
happy to have you here.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:26.190
So I'm going to begin by presenting

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:29.100
work from a book that myself

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.430
and Brenda van Coppenolle,
who's over there,

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.530
are writing on the historical uses

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.110
of lotteries throughout cases in Europe.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:38.460
But I'm first gonna start broader

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.200
by giving you a motivation.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.440
So if you haven't noticed,
democracy is in crisis, right?

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.800
Or at least this is a pervasive
narrative, both in academia

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.260
and in the policy sphere.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.390
This is very much the case in America,

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.370
but also in western democracies in

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:54.960
Europe, all over the place, right?

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.040
So we think that democracy is broken

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:02.580
and increasingly there's
lots of reasons why, right?

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.160
The rise of extremism,
radicalism in politics,

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.350
the decline of parties, strong parties,

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:09.240
and kind traditional party membership.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.700
Campaign finance means that elections are

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.160
increasingly bought by wealthy elites,

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.380
often kind of non-political elites.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.820
There's a lot of corruption.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.560
There's increasingly
democratic backsliding,

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.020
although we're kind of arguing
about when, where, high,

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.150
when, where, why, and how to define it.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.370
And also declining trust in institutions.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:27.990
So across the world, we're seeing people

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:29.070
don't have trust in government.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.330
They don't have trust in politics,

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:33.090
they don't have trust in democracy.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:35.340
And I'm gonna argue
that the real problem is

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.290
that voters neither feel included

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.090
nor represented in a system

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.190
that's increasingly dominated by elites.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:42.870
So everyday voters are,

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.600
are increasingly set apart from democracy.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.330
Okay? So there are lots of ways to fix all

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:50.460
of these problems, but how
do we fix this last one?

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.500
Lotteries increasingly have been

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.120
advocated as a solution, right?

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.250
So these are all kind of recent headlines.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.440
Can randomly selected citizens govern

00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.940
better than elected officials?

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.310
Should we give power to ordinary people?

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.950
Voting undermines the will of the people,

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:07.320
it's time to replace it with sortition.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.270
Okay? And just in case you think

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.880
that this is just kinda a
popular topic for op-eds,

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.760
you know, it's easy to throw
out sortition in lottocracies,

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.760
but, you know, maybe this
isn't a real policy idea.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.740
These are happening all
over the world, right?

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:23.790
So deliberative institutions,
bodies that are selecting

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.490
random people to have an
active role in policymaking,

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:28.740
sometimes consultative, sometimes advisory

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:30.570
are happening all over the world

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:32.430
and have been happening
for quite a long time.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.740
So that's Belgium in 2011.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.250
Paris recently in 2023, east Belgium,

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:41.940
Ireland, a lot of democracies
are now innovating

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:45.330
by selecting citizens and
bringing them into policy,

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.580
typically paired with
deliberative democracy.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.300
Okay? And so what's interesting,

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:55.140
and we think, you know, all of
these innovations are great,

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.210
but Brenda and I set out to write a book

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.040
that's very much in the
historical political

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.600
economy tradition, right?

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.250
And so our book, which is gonna come out

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:04.200
as a Cambridge Elements
in a political economy,

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:05.850
it's called "Lotteries and Democracy".

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:09.480
We are chose to look back
in time at all of the cases

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.080
that throughout history, where
actually governing bodies use

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.690
lotteries in their standing assemblies.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:19.320
And the nice thing is that we
can use the historical cases

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.840
to give us actual empirical
data on how these work,

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.030
how these function, what
worked, what didn't,

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:26.580
why were they put in place,
and why were they dismantled?

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:29.580
And hopefully this can help inform the use

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:33.360
of lotteries in permanent
governing assemblies today.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.880
And in the book, I'm not gonna
present the whole book today,

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:38.850
I'm just gonna give you kind
of a really fun chapter.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.190
But in the book, we assess
kind of whether lotteries

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.890
improve representation and
prevented elite capture.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:47.580
These are kind of the two
main reasons why we advocate

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:49.470
for lotteries or why
policymakers are advocating

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:50.340
for lotteries.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.350
And we also look at things
like implementation,

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.540
which is often missed in
these kind of high level

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:55.410
op-eds, right?

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:58.380
So screening, auditing, what's
the composition of the pool?

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.503
Are there any selection effects
that we need to deal with?

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:06.000
And so today I'm going
to kind of quickly review

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.330
the motivation for why kind
of historical policymakers

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.310
and modern policymakers
want to turn to lotteries.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:14.130
And then we're gonna look
at a natural experiment

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:16.290
of deliberative committees in Denmark.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:19.740
Denmark wrote their constitution
in deliberative committees.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.510
And so we're going to look
to the past to get kind

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:23.760
of cool results about Denmark.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.500
And then I'll conclude. Okay.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:31.620
So definitions, at least, you
know, I told all presenters

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.620
to define their terms
because we all use kind of

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:36.870
similar terminology, but just to make sure

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:38.190
we're on the same page.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:39.660
So we're talking, when
we talk about lotteries,

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.070
we're talking about the selection

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:43.143
of political representatives by lottery.

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:45.840
Sometimes this is called sortition.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.670
You can call this lottocratic selection,

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:49.980
you can call this selection by lot.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:53.340
But note that most modern
uses of lotteries involve

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:55.860
some sort of stratified
random sampling, right?

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:57.810
So we're trying to choose
a representative snapshot

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:01.080
of society, but we are
weighting the random sampling on

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:04.653
things like gender, geographic
location, et cetera.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:08.820
Why do you select people by lottery?

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:12.120
It improves representation
and equalizes access to power.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:14.820
So people that are not elite have a chance

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.440
to be at the table.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:19.530
It's anti-corruption and it
kind of prevents elite capture.

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:21.810
You can't buy off, at least
not before the election.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:23.310
You can't buy off
someone if you don't know

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:25.050
who's going to kinda be chosen.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:26.520
Same with campaign finance.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.170
It eliminates the need
for campaign finance

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.213
if you're choosing officials by lottery.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:33.870
And so these are some of the
reasons why we might want

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:37.860
to use lotteries in
democratic governance, right?

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:40.110
But what's really great, and
this is also kind of a preface

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.720
the entire conference, is
that lotteries can be used

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.720
to select a wide range of
democratic institutions

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:47.130
and in many different ways.

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:48.960
So for you political
scientists that study kind of

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:51.480
comparative institutions,
it's like electoral law.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:55.170
You can slice and dice random
selection at different levels.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.510
You can make it permanent,
you can make it not permanent.

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:59.070
You can have hybrid systems

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:01.740
where you have a sortition
chamber and an elected chamber.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:03.990
There's lots of really interesting ways

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:06.300
to incorporate lotteries
in democratic governance.

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:08.310
And that is actually the
motivation behind this conference.

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:11.070
Today, you're going to see
many, many ways to use lotteries

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.983
and ways that we think
can improve democracy.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:17.153
Yeah. Costas?

00:06:18.283 --> 00:06:19.200
Yeah. Yeah.

