- Okay, great. Thank you
Costas. Great questions.

00:07:34.989 --> 00:07:38.750
So one our lottery's a stable equilibrium

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:42.060
and we actually show in
the book that lotteries,

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:44.130
and this is not a chapter
that I'm going to present,

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:45.630
but it relates to the roles of parties.

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:47.190
I'm gonna discuss the role of parties in

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:49.410
this historical case quite a bit.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:50.243
It is the case

00:07:50.243 --> 00:07:53.220
that we found historically
lotteries work best kind of in

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:55.620
new democracies, in unstable environments

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:58.200
where you don't have strong
parties to control selection.

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:00.840
So it's kind of a rule that
everyone can agree upon

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.630
to organize the society
because it's fair, right?

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:06.630
Lotteries are fair, they're
irrational, you know,

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:08.280
it's not the the strongest

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.410
or someone from this part

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:11.610
of the Italian city states

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:13.950
controlling, you know, the currency.

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:15.390
We like choosing by lottery.

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:16.380
However, we show,

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:18.570
and actually the third
Republican in France is

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:20.220
a great example of this.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:22.380
We have a JOP in a world
politics on this that show

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:24.510
that parties were fine with lotteries

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:26.490
for decades while they were weak.

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:30.060
As soon as parties got strong,
you want to be able to choose

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:31.860
access to committees, you
want to be able to choose

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:33.570
everything so they take them away.

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:36.000
And we argue in the book that
this is actually important

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.430
to realize that it's not
the case that parties,

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:41.190
especially strong parties,
you know, hundreds of years,

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:43.290
thousands of years later, right?

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:46.050
Are won't necessarily allow this rule

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:47.280
to kind of supplant them.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:51.210
And so in the panel we're gonna
talk about the relationship

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:52.890
between parties and lottocracy

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:54.900
and is it going to be a
productive relationship or not?

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:57.210
So no, it's not a stable
equilibrium, at least not

00:08:57.210 --> 00:08:58.980
what we can tell from
the historical cases.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:01.860
And then we do in the book study

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:03.960
what we call lottery based procedures,

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:05.670
which is where you're selecting,

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:07.380
you're using a two stage process,

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:09.540
you split the chamber into random groups.

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:12.030
Those random groups then
elect a representative.

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:14.700
So you don't choose the
representative by lottery,

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:16.470
but you choose the groups by lottery

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:17.550
and then they elect the person.

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:19.740
And even that like kind
of intermediate step,

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:21.570
we show improves representation

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:24.000
and gets people onto committees
that wouldn't have before.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:25.890
So even a little bit of lottery helps,

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:27.420
but I'll go back to that in a second.

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:28.970
Great question, though, Costas.

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:34.260
Okay, so you can, lotteries can be used

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:37.200
to select a wide range of
democratic institutions

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:40.050
and they have been, right?

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:41.760
So the deliberative wave.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:43.080
And what's interesting is, you know,

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:45.900
last night at dinner we were
kind of comparing notes like,

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:47.490
oh, have you heard about the Paris one?

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:49.740
Oh, have you heard about Milan?

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:51.450
Is doing now deliberative democracy?

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:52.560
Have you heard about, you know,

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:55.140
what's going on in New York or California?

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:57.750
This is such a burgeoning field

00:09:57.750 --> 00:09:58.950
that this is actually outdated.

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:01.290
So this is the OECD keeping
track of all these kind

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:02.490
of deliberative initiatives.

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:05.010
And again, deliberate
democracy uses lotteries

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:07.770
to choose the citizens that
are involved in the debate

00:10:07.770 --> 00:10:09.450
coming together to kind of debate policy

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:10.770
and then provide recommendations.

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:12.420
This is only till 2021,

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:14.550
but as you see, you know,
we're at the beginning

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:16.320
of a very steep incline.

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:19.080
So all of these institutions
are something we really need

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:21.510
to pay attention to, right?

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:22.740
Now, what's interesting is that,

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:24.870
so most of these are involving citizens,

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:25.950
although there are some exceptions.

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:27.540
Paris and Belgium are exceptions

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:30.150
where they're incorporating
politicians to work

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:32.820
with citizens in kinda
these liberal committees.

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:33.657
But most of these are citizens

00:10:33.657 --> 00:10:36.060
and a lot of our academic
research are focusing on whether

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:39.690
everyday people learn, have
positive participation,

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:41.310
change their minds, you know,

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:43.620
have more trust in government
after participating.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:45.630
But Brenda and I are actually
interested in the historical

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:47.520
cases where we're at higher levels

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:49.080
with permanent governing assemblies,

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:50.610
with actual politicians, right?

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:52.650
And so that's what our book is focused on.

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:55.230
And prior research also
sometimes is a bit kind

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:57.900
of prescriptive and
hypothetical, which is great.

00:10:57.900 --> 00:10:59.160
We need normative theorists

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:01.560
and philosophers thinking about, you know,

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:03.750
the core definitions of democracy.

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:05.280
But Brenda and I are empiricists.

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:08.070
And so we think that we
can use kind of real data

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:11.160
and statistical analysis to
hope, hopefully shed light on

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:14.220
how lotteries work at
very high levels, right?

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:16.710
And high levels of decision making

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:19.110
with professional politicians,
with political parties,

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:20.820
we don't necessarily think that some

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:23.250
of the great findings
from citizen assemblies

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:24.180
would transfer up.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:25.890
And so for political science professors,

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:28.050
all the political science
professors are nodding.

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:29.970
If you've ever dealt with
politicians like these

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:30.803
might be the slowest to update.

00:11:30.803 --> 00:11:34.380
And so our book looks at
politicians, high levels

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:35.790
with actual governing stakes.

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:38.607
So they were choosing the
currency, they were choosing

00:11:38.607 --> 00:11:41.910
the budget, how the budget
would be allocated in France,

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:45.330
they were writing the constitution, okay?

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:48.690
And Costas, your lottery based procedures

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:49.740
show up in the book.

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:52.050
They show up in the 19th
century French case.

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:54.300
The cases that we look
at, we look at a lot

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:55.530
of different historical cases

00:11:55.530 --> 00:11:57.330
that each use lotteries
in a different way.

00:11:57.330 --> 00:11:59.340
So the book is pretty expansive.

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:01.200
We look at ancient Athens,
which was, you know,

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:03.300
more of a lottocracy where
they're actually selecting

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:04.290
the officials.

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:05.670
We look at medieval Italy

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:08.700
where they chose the
standard bearer in Florence.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:11.280
They were choosing, again,
economic kind of leaders.

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:14.100
Denmark and France. We
look at constitutions.

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.950
So France and Denmark wrote
their constitutions in

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:19.470
deliberative groups like,
you know, kind of mimicking

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:22.290
the citizens' assemblies that
have provided advisory powers.

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:25.710
But again, these are politicians
writing the constitution

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:27.930
and then the lottery based
procedures in 19th century

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:30.780
Europe, which is this two
stage selecting committees.

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:33.600
So I'm gonna talk about Denmark today,

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:35.610
but importantly our book
shows that there's a lot

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:38.250
of different ways to
incorporate lotteries.

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:39.930
And again, our big findings are

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:41.310
that generally they do improve

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:43.110
representation if you can keep them.

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:47.940
Okay, I kind of went over
this, so I'll go quickly,

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:50.280
but we do overall, we do find that kind

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:52.770
of across the cases it
does lotteries do improve

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:55.770
representation, but it
also depends on the pool.

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:58.320
So one thing that's
kind of missing from all

00:12:58.320 --> 00:12:59.280
of these policymakers

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:00.930
and all of these op-eds
saying we should go back

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:04.800
to a sortition, if you want to
undermine lotteries, you mess

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:06.600
with the pool that they're
originally chosen from.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:07.770
And that's what Greece did, right?

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:10.080
So ancient Athens, the
pool was restrictive,

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:12.270
it was not everyone in
the polity, you know,

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:13.890
it was effectively elites.

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:16.530
So if you restrict the pool
and then randomly select,

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:17.363
you don't get the,

00:13:17.363 --> 00:13:19.350
necessarily the benefits of
increased representation,

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:21.750
although it can still be kind
of anti-corruption, right?

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:23.610
But, so we need to take a step back

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:25.350
and that's some of the projects
I'm working on this year

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:27.840
while I'm visiting Yale to
think about self-selection

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:30.240
to lotteries and think about
how to make the pool expansive

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:32.250
but also tractable to
kind of scale up, right?

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:33.083
Yeah. Kevin.

00:13:33.083 --> 00:13:36.810
- [Kevin] Sorry, Kevin
Elliot, Yale and (indistinct).

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:40.200
So when you mean represented
represented here, you mean

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:42.150
the groups that are selected will look

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:44.070
more like the body as a
whole, is that correct?

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:46.173
- Yeah, and in particular, like
trying to think about groups

00:13:46.173 --> 00:13:48.690
that would not normally be
in politics if, you know,

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:51.723
typical elites and
elections were in place yet.

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:54.780
Anyway, so pay attention
to the pool, right?

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:56.670
It's, again, you can have a great policy

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:59.040
but if you mess with a
step before the policy,

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:01.680
it can undermine the
benefits of the policy.

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:03.960
Yep, it does prevent elite capture,

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:05.940
but strong parties will
dismantle lotteries,

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:07.680
at least in the night in Europe.

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:08.910
That's what happened.

00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:10.650
And yes, we can learn from implementation.

00:14:10.650 --> 00:14:15.270
So things like screening,
auditing, you know, giving

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:17.520
individual representatives
chosen by lottery help

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:19.770
and support, all of that would
be important if you actually

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:22.380
want to bring lotteries up
to governing assemblies.

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:24.270
But anyway, so that's kind of the preview

00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:25.770
of the findings of the book.

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:28.560
But today, for the next 15 minutes or so,

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:30.840
I'm going to talk about
whether a historical case can

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:32.310
teach us about deliberative committees

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:33.540
and deliberative committees
are when you're kind of

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:36.270
bringing citizens and
politicians to debate,

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:38.640
discuss, and make policy recommendations.

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:39.810
But policy recommendations

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:43.830
that will influence policy
in, are more advisory.

00:14:43.830 --> 00:14:45.000
So we're gonna look at the creation

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:46.620
of the June constitution in Denmark.

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:49.710
This is the constitution that
governs Denmark to this day.

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:51.060
So if we're thinking about high stakes,

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:52.830
this was not a temporary document.

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:55.590
They wrote the, this document
existed, it's been amended

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:57.780
of course, but in these
deliberative groups

00:14:57.780 --> 00:15:00.060
and we're gonna exploit
a natural experiment

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:01.166
that created these groups

00:15:01.166 --> 00:15:02.370
and we're gonna see if they did anything.

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:04.140
And we're gonna measure
whether they did anything

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:06.540
by looking at voting on
constitutional amendments.

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:07.950
So did people get more democratic

00:15:07.950 --> 00:15:09.450
as they deliberated into this groups?

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:12.660
And we test whether kinda
this group assignment thing,

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:14.490
you have this group
assignment as a treatment

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:17.100
affected your legislative behavior.

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:18.900
And another great thing about this case is

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:22.140
that obviously we have no
survey measures self-reported.

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:23.310
Did you have a great time

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:25.080
Danish politicians in the 19th century?

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:26.010
Delivery of these groups.

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:26.843
We can't do that.

00:15:26.843 --> 00:15:28.110
But we have what's even better,

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:29.670
which is we have the votes,

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:31.590
which would be consequential, right?

00:15:31.590 --> 00:15:34.500
You can say you had a great
time in a deliberative exercise

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:35.700
and then not change your mind,

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:37.350
your behavior, or your attitudes, right?

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:39.750
And I'm not saying that's
happening in modern day citizens

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:41.340
assemblies, but the nice thing is

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:43.950
that we have actual costly outcomes

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:45.903
to measure using this historical case.

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:49.530
And then we're kind of speaking

00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:52.350
to theories about
deliberation in mixed groups.

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:54.360
This is a very short list,

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:56.370
but you know, there have
been mixed findings.

00:15:56.370 --> 00:15:58.620
Sometimes you think that if
you get into deliberative

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:00.630
groups, you're exposed
to different viewpoints

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:02.250
and you moderate, you learn,

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:04.260
you become less polarized, less cohesive.

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:05.850
We've had experimental work show this

00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:07.710
and we've had observational
work show this.

00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:09.660
But it's also the case
that, you know, exposures

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:11.700
to different viewpoints might reinforce

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:13.140
your existing opinions, right?

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:14.940
You might double down or just might

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:16.230
not change your opinions.

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:19.110
And again, we have very few studies that

00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:20.850
use actual politicians

00:16:20.850 --> 00:16:23.910
and the studies that do use
actual politicians in Ireland

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:26.160
where they've had deliberative
committees before,

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:28.890
often show that politicians
are kind of less likely

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:30.570
to moderate and less likely to change

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:32.070
because they have
different concerns, right?

00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:33.660
They're very concerned
about public relations

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:37.950
and the PR consequences of

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:39.810
participating in these
deliberative committees.

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:42.600
But again, does that translate
into actual policy change

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:44.760
or do they just fall
back to partisan lines?

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:46.950
There's, you know, they're less kind

00:16:46.950 --> 00:16:48.840
of happy results when you use politicians.

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:52.500
So these are the literature
that we're speaking to, okay?

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:55.800
Denmark, 19th century. So
that's the constituent assembly.

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:57.960
Obviously all of our politicians are men

00:16:57.960 --> 00:16:59.313
because, you know, history.

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:03.270
Okay, Denmark was kinda
an interesting case

00:17:03.270 --> 00:17:04.320
of democratization.

00:17:04.320 --> 00:17:08.880
So Europe was facing a wave
of revolutions in 1848,

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:10.380
Denmark was at war.

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:12.900
The Danish king saw the
way the wind was blowing

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:15.030
and decided to cede the country

00:17:15.030 --> 00:17:17.550
to create a democratic republic.

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:19.800
He was called Frederick the
Beloved because of this.

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:21.420
So he did very well in history.

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:23.880
But it's a bit unusual case
that Denmark didn't get kind

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:26.490
of toppled by a revolution
like France did.

00:17:26.490 --> 00:17:28.530
The Danish king was like, "Uh uh, okay,

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:30.300
let's create a constituent assembly.

00:17:30.300 --> 00:17:32.190
You know, I'll stay a
constitutional monarch,

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:34.590
but let's create a constitution," right?

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:36.150
So this happened in 1848

00:17:36.150 --> 00:17:38.280
and the constitution was adopted in 1849.

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:39.990
So we're talking really about a year

00:17:39.990 --> 00:17:43.080
and kind of a smaller period
for deliberation voting.

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:45.900
And there's a lot of, there's
some good work on kind of

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:47.160
roll call voting in the case.

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:49.650
But generally there's not a
lot of studies on this case,

00:17:49.650 --> 00:17:51.510
which maybe because it's not conflict

00:17:51.510 --> 00:17:53.580
or war oriented, it was
a peaceful transition.

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:57.600
So everyone studies France
and not Denmark. Okay?

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:00.120
So the parties, parties are
gonna play an important role

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:01.260
and this is a great case

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:03.480
because the parties
were weak but existing.

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:08.250
So they stood on ideological platforms

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:10.350
or they had labels for
themselves when they ran

00:18:10.350 --> 00:18:12.210
to be elected to the constituent assembly.

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:15.180
But luckily for us, the
blocks are pretty traditional.

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:16.650
19th century Europe, you know,

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:18.870
the left was a movement of rich farmers.

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:21.180
They were not peasants,
but they wanted democracy,

00:18:21.180 --> 00:18:22.740
they wanted representation.

00:18:22.740 --> 00:18:24.360
The national liberals were the ones

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:25.740
that actually pushed for the constitution.

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:27.450
These are kinda the
merchants in the cities.

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:29.970
They want democracy, but
they kind of want to run it.

00:18:29.970 --> 00:18:31.350
They don't want the
rich farmers to run it.

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:33.180
So they're like medium pro-democracy.

00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:35.760
And then you have the right,
which are aristocratic,

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:37.200
buddies with the king.

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:39.240
Everyone has accepted that
we need to have a democracy.

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:42.480
But there's a spectrum on how
inclusive, how democratic,

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.550
how much the king has
power to really kind of

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:45.660
dissolve the assembly.

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:47.280
And so that's where
they're gonna be fighting

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:48.843
over in the constitution.

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:53.730
Okay? And parties were
parties, institutions were new

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:55.050
and weak, right?

00:18:55.050 --> 00:18:57.720
So they said, "Hey, let's
look at what France did.

00:18:57.720 --> 00:19:00.690
France had a solution for kind
of an unstable environment

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:03.393
and that's how France
did their constitution.

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:05.393
Let's do that."

00:19:05.393 --> 00:19:07.530
And so anti-democratic elites,

00:19:07.530 --> 00:19:09.390
they were worried about
kind of blocking democracy.

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:11.670
So they decided to use lotteries.

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:13.380
So they used these random
deliberative groups

00:19:13.380 --> 00:19:15.120
and the rationale, and we can see

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:16.680
that from the parliamentary debate.

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:18.330
So why did they put this rule in place?

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:20.730
It was to expose members
to crosscut debate,

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:23.460
to make people get into a
room with other parties,

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:25.110
other members that they didn't know

00:19:25.110 --> 00:19:26.520
to let ideas flow better.

00:19:26.520 --> 00:19:31.080
So exactly the same rationales
we have today. Okay?

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:34.440
So our constituent assembly has 157

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:35.820
elected or king appointed members.

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:37.020
Five of them went off to be minister.

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:40.680
So it ends up being 152. We
have five deliberative groups.

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:43.290
So they deliberate in groups of 30 or 31,

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:44.460
which is relatively large.

00:19:44.460 --> 00:19:46.410
But they stayed in these
groups the whole time, right?

00:19:46.410 --> 00:19:48.360
So we're gonna kind of
exploit these groups

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:51.180
to see if over these kind of
six months there was learning,

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:53.250
they debated the constitution draft

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:55.650
and then we have the roll call
votes in the general assembly

00:19:55.650 --> 00:19:59.220
where they voted on each of
these parts of the constitution.

00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:00.630
And again, three parties.

00:20:00.630 --> 00:20:03.000
And then the king was
kind of a background.

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:05.490
The king actually appointed
deputies to make sure

00:20:05.490 --> 00:20:08.040
that there were folks
protecting his interests

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:10.110
because the election for
the constituent assembly

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:11.550
went really left.

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:12.900
But because the king interfered,

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:14.280
you basically have equal numbers

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:16.053
of each block across the chamber.

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:19.200
Okay? So what we're trying

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:21.690
to ask using this case
is do po-, you know,

00:20:21.690 --> 00:20:24.990
does policy deliberation in
randomly assigned groups affect

00:20:24.990 --> 00:20:27.360
voting behavior on the draft constitution

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:30.270
with the hypothesis is that
if these randomly group,

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:32.160
randomly assigned groups expose members

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:35.430
to these new viewpoints, we
should see behavioral change.

00:20:35.430 --> 00:20:38.820
The way we decide to
operationalize this is

00:20:38.820 --> 00:20:42.060
by looking at the number
of left wing deputies

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:43.440
or here I'm gonna show you the share

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:45.900
of left wing deputies in
your group with this idea

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:48.060
that they were the most democratic.

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:51.000
You know, if you were a
liberal or a right wing deputy

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:54.330
and you were exposed to more
of these left wing deputies,

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:55.980
you would maybe move to closer to them.

00:20:55.980 --> 00:20:58.410
That is a choice, that
is a substantive choice.

00:20:58.410 --> 00:21:00.797
We also look at the number of
right-wing deputies, right?

00:21:00.797 --> 00:21:04.290
'cause you might be affected
by the other tales. Yeah, Kevin

00:21:04.290 --> 00:21:07.410
- [Kevin] Was universal men in suffrage.

00:21:07.410 --> 00:21:09.865
Was that, was that on the table?

00:21:09.865 --> 00:21:13.076
- Yeah, that some of the
votes that we have were

00:21:13.076 --> 00:21:15.630
the titling land
requirements for suffrage.

00:21:15.630 --> 00:21:17.910
And so, and it works
exactly like you think,

00:21:17.910 --> 00:21:21.247
the rich farmers wanted
inclusive suffrage and then,

00:21:21.247 --> 00:21:25.290
you know, the right wanted like
only very, very rich elite.

00:21:25.290 --> 00:21:29.340
Yeah. So they have like a
series of votes, which you know,

00:21:29.340 --> 00:21:31.560
spoiler alert, the rich farmers lost.

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:36.480
Yeah. Okay. So we use a lot
of cool historical data.

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:38.190
So we use the parliamentary records,

